Glossarion overlay of flet-dev/mobile-forge recipes/llama-cpp-python/patches/mobile.patch
(0fadbfab0d63d11164030dfddc955ed980e32e2c, BSD-3-Clause, see ../../LICENSE.mobile-forge):
upstream's five changes below, unchanged, plus a sixth one for the vision build (LLAVA_BUILD=ON),
a seventh one for the runtime CPU variants of the Android arm64 build, an eighth one for the
optional GPU backends, a ninth one for the DRY sampler and a tenth one for the optional NPU
backend:

6. Drops SONAME versioning from libmtmd too (single unversioned libmtmd, like the others).

7. Runtime CPU-variant selection (meta.yaml builds Android arm64 with GGML_BACKEND_DL=ON and
   GGML_CPU_ALL_VARIANTS=ON):
   - ggml/src/CMakeLists.txt: the Android variant list is cut to the four NEON feature sets
     android_armv8.0_1 (baseline), android_armv8.2_1 (dotprod), android_armv8.2_2 (dotprod, fp16)
     and android_armv8.6_1 (dotprod, fp16, i8mm); no SVE / SVE2 / SME variants. The option
     GLOSSARION_ANDROID_CPU_VARIANTS builds the same set on Linux arm64 (the CI check).
   - _ctypes_extensions.py: after preloading libggml, register_cpu_backend() asks every variant
     library for its ggml_backend_score() (the feature probe; 0 = the CPU lacks a feature) and
     registers the best with ggml_backend_load(), loading by bare soname from the APK's jniLibs
     where no file exists (ggml's own ggml_backend_load_all() only scans the executable's
     directory and the cwd). CPU_BACKEND_VARIANT names the registered variant ("builtin" when
     libggml links the CPU backend itself: x86_64, iOS).

8. Optional GPU backends, registered only when the app asks (its "Use GPU" setting, off by
   default; meta.yaml builds Android arm64 with GGML_OPENCL=ON and iOS with GGML_METAL=ON):
   - _ctypes_extensions.py: register_gpu_backend() loads libggml-opencl.so with
     ggml_backend_load() (Android arm64: the only library that needs the vendor's libOpenCL.so,
     so a phone without OpenCL just gets an error string) or registers the linked Metal backend
     with ggml_backend_register(ggml_backend_metal_reg()) (iOS), and reports the first usable
     GPU device or why there is none (GPU_STATE). The preload chain loads libggml-metal before
     libggml where it exists. gpu_backend_name() says which backend the build ships, loading
     nothing.
   - ggml/src/ggml-backend-reg.cpp: the registry no longer registers Metal when it starts, so
     Metal's device setup and runtime shader compilation never run while the GPU is off
     (GLOSSARION_GGML_EAGER_METAL=1 restores upstream's behaviour).

9. DRY sampler: llama.py's _init_sampler adds llama.cpp's DRY sampler (LlamaSampler.add_dry,
   already bound) after the penalties when the Llama instance carries a dry_sampling dict
   (multiplier, base, allowed_length, penalty_last_n, seq_breakers) with a multiplier above 0;
   create_chat_completion has no DRY parameters, so the app sets the attribute per request.

10. Optional NPU backend (Qualcomm Hexagon HTP, ggml-hexagon), registered only when the app asks
    (its NPU check; meta.yaml builds Android arm64 with GGML_HEXAGON=ON when wheelhouse.toml
    [hexagon] enabled = true, through the CMake initial cache wheels.py stage-hexagon writes):
    - ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/CMakeLists.txt: the HTP skeletons (Hexagon DSP ELF files, one per arch
      v73/v75/v79/v81/v85) install as package data llama_cpp/htp/libggml-htp-vNN.so.dsp instead of
      into llama_cpp/lib (no ".so" ending: they stay in sitepackages.zip, out of jniLibs and Gradle's
      strip step). libggml-hexagon.so itself is a GGML_BACKEND_DL module next to libggml-opencl.
      Each skeleton sub-build starts from empty base compiler/linker flags, so forge's Android
      CFLAGS/LDFLAGS never reach hexagon-clang.
    - ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/ggml-hexagon.cpp: glossarion_hexagon_session_check(), an exported C
      function that opens a device's real session through ggml_backend_dev_init() and returns the
      C++ exception text instead of letting it cross the C ABI (sessions open lazily; a cDSP that
      refuses the unsigned PD only shows there).
    - _ctypes_extensions.py: npu_backend_shipped() (the skeletons are in the package, loads
      nothing); register_npu_backend(skel_dir, options) (once per process, never raises): loads
      libcdsprpc.so, asks the cDSP's arch itself (DSPRPC_GET_DSP_INFO, as htp-drv.cpp does, so
      upstream's silent v73 fallback never decides), copies the skeletons to skel_dir as
      libggml-htp-vNN.so, sets GGML_HEXAGON_ARCH, the caller's GGML_HEXAGON_* options and
      GGML_HEXAGON_LIBHTP_DIR before the only ggml_backend_load("libggml-hexagon.so"), then runs
      the session check; it reports each step (shipped, libcdsprpc, arch, skeletons, registry,
      session). device_index(name) is the main_gpu of a registered backend ("HTP", "OpenCL") in
      llama.cpp's own device order (llama_prepare_model_devices). The GPU path shares the backend
      loading helpers (_load_backend, _beside_libggml, _android_arm64).

Upstream's description:

It does five things:

1. Stops llama-cpp-python's CMakeLists from FORCE-enabling GGML_METAL on all
   Apple platforms (it would override the recipe's own GGML_METAL / embed flags for
   the iOS cross build and guess the arch with `uname -m`).
2. Skips building llama.cpp's unused `common` helper lib (~5 MB).
3. Drops SONAME versioning from the shipped libs (ggml-base/ggml/ggml-cpu/
   llama) so the wheel carries single unversioned files instead of a
   lib*.dylib -> .0 -> .0.15.3 symlink triplet that forge dereferences into
   three copies (and three colliding frameworks on iOS).
4. Teaches the ctypes loader to load the bundled libs on iOS, where serious-python
   relocates each dylib into a code-signed <name>.framework and leaves a
   lib<name>.fwork TEXT marker (its content is the app-relative framework-binary
   path). The loader now RESOLVES that marker to the real framework binary (as
   CPython's AppleFrameworkLoader does — join the marker text above the wheel dir)
   before ctypes.CDLL, instead of trying to dlopen the text marker itself; and it
   preloads the ggml chain in dependency order (ggml-base -> ggml-cpu -> ggml ->
   llama) with RTLD_GLOBAL. serious-python leaves the dylibs' install-ids
   unchanged (@rpath/lib<name>.dylib), so a preloaded framework satisfies the next
   lib's sibling @rpath reference by install-id. Also handles sys.platform == "ios"
   (flet's 3.13+).
5. Android/Flet 0.86: site-packages ships as a zip and serious-python relocates
   the bundled libs (libllama/libggml*) into the APK's jniLibs, so the loader's
   Path.exists() probes against the in-zip base_path all miss. Add a bare-soname
   fallback (ctypes.CDLL("lib<name>.so")) for both the dependency preload and the
   main load, so the Android linker resolves them from jniLibs.

